input\ninput: {\n    "question": "<p>I have a toy model:</p>\\n<pre><code>import torch\\nimport torch.nn as nn\\nimport torch.optim as optim\\n\\nclass Model(nn.Module):\\n    def __init__(self):\\n        super(Model, self).__init__()\\n        self.fc1 = nn.Linear(1, 2)\\n        self.fc2 = nn.Linear(2, 3)\\n        self.fc3 = nn.Linear(3, 1)\\n\\n    def forward(self, x):\\n        x = self.fc1(x)\\n        x = torch.relu(x)        \\n        x = torch.relu(self.fc2(x))\\n        x = self.fc3(x)\\n        return x\\n\\nnet = Model()\\n\\nopt = optim.Adam(net.parameters())\\n</code></pre>\\n<p>The training loop is</p>\\n<pre><code>features = torch.rand((3,1))\\nfor i in range(10):\\n    opt.zero_grad()\\n    out = net(features)\\n    loss = torch.mean(torch.square(torch.tensor(5) - torch.sum(out)))\\n    loss.backward()\\n    opt.step()\\n</code></pre>\\n<p>How can I check if any of the gradients is nan? That is, if just 1 of the gradients is nan print something/break</p>\\n<p><strong>pseudocode:</strong></p>\\n<pre><code>for i in range(10):\\n    opt.zero_grad()\\n    out = net(features)\\n    loss = torch.mean(torch.square(torch.tensor(5) - torch.sum(out)))\\n    loss.backward()\\n\\n    if_gradients_nan:\\n        print(&quot;NAN&quot;)\\n\\n    opt.step()\\n</code></pre>",\n    "answer": "I\'m afraid there\'s no way to do this without writing your own custom autograd function. The reason for this is that the gradient computation is done internally by PyTorch, and you cannot access it directly from Python. You could try using `autograd.grad`, but I think that would be very inefficient.\\n\\nHere\'s how you could implement such a custom autograd function:\\n\\n```\\nfrom torch.autograd import Function\\n\\nclass NanToNum(Function):\\n\\n    @staticmethod\\n    def forward(ctx, input, nan=None, posinf=None, neginf=None):\\n        ctx.save_for_backward(input)\\n        result = torch.nan_to_num(input, nan, posinf, neginf)\\n        return result\\n\\n    @staticmethod\\n    def backward(ctx, grad_output):\\n        # Get saved tensors\\n        input, = ctx.saved_tensors\\n\\n        # Compute mask\\n        mask = ~torch.isnan(input)\\n\\n        # Mask gradients\\n        grad_input = grad_output.clone()\\n        grad_input[mask == False] = 0\\n\\n        return grad_input, None, None, None\\n```\\n\\nAnd here\'s how you could use it:\\n\\n```\\nfeatures = torch.rand((3,1))\\nfor i in range(10):\\n    opt.zero_grad()\\n    out = net(features)\\n    loss = torch.mean(torch.square(torch.tensor(5) - torch.sum(out)))\\n    loss.backward()\\n\\n    # Check if gradients contain NaNs\\n    if NanToNum.apply(loss).isnan().any():\\n        print(\\"NAN\\")\\n\\n    opt.step()\\n```\\n\\nNote that this implementation will work correctly only if all the parameters of the network are trainable. If some of them are fixed, you\'ll need to modify the implementation accordingly."\n}\nOutput: ', 'role': 'user'}], 'model': 'Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct', 'n': 1, 'stream': False, 'temperature': 0.01}}
