rad`, but I think that would be very inefficient.\\n\\nHere\'s how you could implement such a custom autograd function:\\n\\n```\\nfrom torch.autograd import Function\\n\\nclass NanToNum(Function):\\n\\n    @staticmethod\\n    def forward(ctx, input, nan=None, posinf=None, neginf=None):\\n        ctx.save_for_backward(input)\\n        result = torch.nan_to_num(input, nan, posinf, neginf)\\n        return result\\n\\n    @staticmethod\\n    def backward(ctx, grad_output):\\n        # Get saved tensors\\n        input, = ctx.saved_tensors\\n\\n        # Compute mask\\n        mask = ~torch.isnan(input)\\n\\n        # Mask gradients\\n        grad_input = grad_output.clone()\\n        grad_input[mask == False] = 0\\n\\n        return grad_input, None, None, None\\n```\\n\\nAnd here\'s how you could use it:\\n\\n```\\nfeatures = torch.rand((3,1))\\nfor i in range(10):\\n    opt.zero_grad()\\n    out = net(features)\\n    loss = torch.mean(torch.square(torch.tensor(5) - torch.sum(out)))\\n    loss.backward()\\n\\n    # Check if gradients contain NaNs\\n    if NanToNum.apply(loss).isnan().any():\\n        print(\\"NAN\\")\\n\\n    opt.step()\\n```\\n\\nNote that this implementation will work correctly only if all the parameters of the network are trainable. If some of them are fixed, you\'ll need to modify the implementation accordingly."\n}\nOutput: ', 'role': 'user'}], 'model': 'Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct', 'n': 3, 'stream': False, 'temperature': 0.3}}
