ef sin(x):\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Args:\\n        x (torch.Tensor): Input tensor\\n\\n    Returns:\\n        torch.Tensor: Output tensor\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    return torch.sin(x)\\n\\n@torch.library.register_kernel(\\"mylib::sin\\", \\"cpu\\")\\ndef _(x):\\n    return torch.sin(x)\\n\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Autograd Function API \\n\\nWarning \\n\\nThe Autograd Function API is a very low-level building block. It is recommended to build your model using [autograd](autograd.html#autograd-doc)  and [nn modules](torch.nn.html#nn-doc)  ,\\nand only use this API if the higher level tools aren’t sufficient for your use case.\\n\\nIn general, you should prefer using the autograd functionality\\nprovided by [autograd](autograd.html#autograd-doc)  . However, there are some situations where it is necessary to use the Autograd\\nFunction API directly. This section describes how to do this. \\n\\nTo create a custom autograd function, you need to subclass `torch.autograd.Function`  and override the `forward()`  and `backward()`  staticmethods. \\n\\nAs you might expect, the `forward()`  method is going to be called every time\\nthe function is invoked during training, and it needs to return the result of\\nthe computation. You can use any combination of CPU/GPU tensors, but they MUST\\nbe passed through `ctx`  (we’ll explain why shortly) and returned as lists.\\nThis is what the `ctx`  variable is for – it provides storage for arbitrary\\ntensors that you would like to use in the backward pass. \\n\\nHere is a simple (but somewhat trivial) example showing how to implement a\\nno-op function: \\n\\n```\\nimport torch\\nfrom torch.autograd import Function\\n\\nclass MyNoOpFunction(Function):\\n\\n    @staticmethod\\n    def forward(ctx, x):\\n        return x\\n\\n    @staticmethod\\n    def backward(ctx, grad_output):\\n        return grad_output\\n\\nx = torch.rand(5, requires_grad=True)\\ny = MyNoOpFunction.apply(x)\\ny.sum().backward()\\n\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\ntorch.Tensor.backwar